        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "What's the difference between Imperial IR-G, IHR, and HD Pro Series?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "Three duty tiers across the Imperial range lineup. IR-G is the standard restaurant-range workhorse — gas burners on top with oven compartment below, sized across burner counts (IR-4, IR-6, IR-8, IR-10, IR-12) for mid-volume LA restaurant back-of-house. IHR is the heavy-duty variant with reinforced construction for higher-volume operations. Pro Series HD24-HD60 is the modular heavy-duty cooking suite — open-top burners, fry-tops, char-grill modules in 24-inch through 60-inch widths for high-volume specialty restaurants. Deep range coverage on our /brands/imperial-range-repair/ combo page; this pillar focuses on the broader product lineup beyond ranges."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "Does Imperial make convection ovens, salamanders, and pasta cookers too?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "Yes. Imperial's full lineup spans six categories beyond ranges. ICV-G and ICV-E convection ovens (single and double-stack — deep coverage on our /brands/imperial-oven-repair/ combo). ISB infrared salamanders for restaurant finishing and browning stations. ICM cheesemelters — heavily concentrated at LA Mexican/Latin restaurants and pizza operations. IRB radiant gas charbroilers + ICRA char-radiant variants. ITG/ITHG griddles (24 to 48-inch flat-tops, gas and electric). IPA pasta cookers — the LA Italian restaurant signature equipment. Plus IFS fryers, range hoods, and the specialty configurations (taco ranges, wok ranges, stock pot ranges) that other manufacturers don't bother with."
