        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "My Imperial cheesemelter (ICM) won't heat — common failure?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "Yes — ICM cheesemelter infrared element burnout is the most common Imperial cheesemelter service call we see in LA. Typical failure at year 4 to 8 in heavy Mexican/Latin restaurant duty (high-cycle cheese-melting workflow accelerates element wear). Diagnosis is straightforward: open-circuit test on the infrared element, then replace with OEM. Most ICM repairs land $240 to $420 parts-and-labor. We carry ICM elements on the van given how frequent these service calls run in the LA Mexican/Latin restaurant install base. Same diagnostic approach overlaps with ISB salamander infrared elements — same family, same wear pattern, different mounting."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "What does Imperial repair cost in LA?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "$120 flat commercial diagnostic, waived when you approve the repair. Most Imperial service calls land between $280 and $650 parts-and-labor. Typical category pricing: SIT thermocouple $180-$320, burner orifice cleaning $180-$280 per burner, burner ignitor $220-$380, gas valve $260-$440, oven thermostat $200-$340, ICV convection fan motor $280-$480, ISB/ICM infrared element $240-$420, IRB charbroiler radiant ceramic $280-$480, ITG griddle thermostat calibration $200-$340, IPA pasta cooker solenoid $240-$380. Imperial repair pricing runs typically 15 to 25 percent below equivalent Vulcan or Garland repair categories — same value-tier positioning that defines Imperial at purchase pricing carries through to service economics."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "Will you service used Imperial bought at restaurant auction?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "Yes. LA's restaurant turnover rate produces a steady stream of used Imperial equipment hitting auction and liquidation channels — Restaurant Equipment World, IRS auctions, RestaurantEquipment.com, plus the direct-dealer-liquidation channel. We service used Imperial regardless of purchase source. Used Imperial economics often work out favorably: used IR-G ranges run $800 to $2,800 (versus $4,000 to $8,000 new), used ICV convection $1,200 to $3,500 (versus $5,000 to $10,000 new), used ICM cheesemelters $400 to $1,200 (versus $1,800 to $4,000 new). Parts availability via Corona factory remains good even on 10 to 12 year-old units. The honest framing: we don't sell Imperial. We service it, and we'll give you a straight assessment after the $120 diagnostic on whether the repair makes economic sense."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "Why won't my Imperial IR-G range burner or oven stay lit?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "The most common cause our techs find is the SIT thermocouple, the flame-safety device that senses the pilot or burner flame and shuts the gas off if it weakens, so a worn one makes the burner or IR-G oven die out shortly after lighting; replacement runs $180 to $320. The next causes are a clogged burner orifice, where carbon and grease narrow the opening and weaken the flame ($180 to $280 per burner), and a gas valve out of spec ($260 to $440). We test the flame and the thermocouple signal before deciding which it is, and the Corona parts proximity keeps most of these single-visit. Same Day Appliance Repair, $120 commercial diagnostic, waived with repair."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "What fails most on an Imperial charbroiler or griddle?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "On an IRB charbroiler the signature item is the radiant ceramic, which degrades under high-heat duty and drops the sear; replacement runs $280 to $480, and a clogged orifice or out-of-spec gas valve can produce the same cool-grate symptom for less. On an ITG griddle the most common call is the thermostat drifting out of calibration ($200 to $340), so the surface runs hot or cold against the setpoint, along with burner-orifice and ignitor work on the gas side. Our techs verify the actual surface or grate temperature against the setpoint before quoting. Same Day Appliance Repair, $120 commercial diagnostic, waived with repair."
        }
      }
    ]
  }
];
