<!-- ── INTRO ── -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="eyebrow-sub">Imperial service across LA</p>
    <h2>The California-made commercial cooking authority with Middleby family scale</h2>
    <p>When an Imperial goes down in an LA kitchen, the failures our techs at Same Day Appliance Repair run most are the gas-control and specialty-element items across the broad lineup: a SIT thermocouple, a clogged burner orifice, a burner ignitor, and a gas valve on the IR-G and IHR ranges, plus the oven thermostat. The signature LA call is the ICM cheesemelter infrared element, which burns out at year 4-to-8 in the heavy Mexican and Latin restaurant cheese-melting duty, and the ISB salamander shares the same infrared wear pattern. On ICV convection ovens it's the fan motor, on IRB charbroilers the radiant ceramic, on ITG griddles the thermostat, and on IPA pasta cookers the solenoid. We service the full lineup on one Middleby sibling-call (Pitco, Frymaster, Blodgett, TurboChef, Wells) with Corona-factory parts proximity. Typical work runs $180-$320 for a SIT thermocouple, $240-$420 for an ICM/ISB infrared element, $280-$480 for an ICV fan motor or IRB radiant, and $260-$440 for a gas valve, most calls $280-$650, 15-to-25% below Vulcan or Garland. Commercial diagnostic is $120, waived with the repair, BHGS #A49573, EPA 608 Universal certified (#1346255700410).</p>
    <p>Across the Imperial equipment our techs service in LA, the failures cluster by category. On the IR-G and IHR ranges, SIT thermocouples lead, then burner-orifice cleaning, burner ignitors, gas valves, and the oven thermostat. The ICM cheesemelter infrared element is the single most frequent specialty call we run, the LA Mexican and Latin restaurant install base cycles them hard, so we carry ICM elements on the van, and the ISB salamander element shares the family. ICV convection fan motors, IRB charbroiler radiant ceramics, ITG griddle thermostats, and IPA pasta-cooker solenoids fill the category-specific mid-band. Control boards are the last and most expensive. Because Imperial ships from Corona, our techs keep most of these single-visit, and the value-tier pricing carries through to service, repairs run 15-to-25% below an equivalent Vulcan or Garland, which keeps even a used auction Imperial on the repair side of the math.</p>
    <p class="intro-speakable">Imperial Commercial Cooking Equipment is part of Middleby Corporation's family of foodservice brands. Founded 1957 in Los Angeles, Imperial manufactures in Corona, California today — roughly 60 miles from downtown LA, and that California factory remains operational under the Middleby umbrella. That combination — Middleby parent scale plus California-retained manufacturing — is structurally rare for a consolidator brand, and it shapes who LA operators actually call when Imperial equipment needs service. We have built our Imperial practice around covering the full product lineup (six categories beyond just ranges and ovens), running the Middleby sibling-call workflow (Imperial + Pitco + Frymaster + Blodgett + TurboChef + Wells on one service account), and leveraging the Corona California parts proximity for fast turnaround on the $120 commercial diagnostic schedule, waived when you approve the repair.</p>
    <p>The Imperial lineup runs deep — IR-G and IHR restaurant ranges, Pro Series HD24-HD60 heavy-duty modular cooking suites, ICV convection ovens, ISB infrared salamanders, ICM cheesemelters, IRB radiant gas charbroilers, ICRA char-radiant variants, ITG griddles in gas and ED electric, IPA pasta cookers, IFS fryers, range hoods, plus the cuisine-specialty configurations that other manufacturers don't bother with (taco ranges sized for tortilla production, wok ranges with proper Cantonese BTU output, stock pot ranges for Asian soup operations). This pillar covers the broader Imperial brand — for deep range coverage we maintain <a href="/brands/imperial-range-repair/">/brands/imperial-range-repair/</a>, and for deep oven coverage <a href="/brands/imperial-oven-repair/">/brands/imperial-oven-repair/</a>. This page extends both with salamanders, charbroilers, griddles, pasta cookers, and the brand-level operational context.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ── OWNERSHIP / MIDDLEBY FAMILY ── -->
<section class="section section-alt">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="eyebrow-sub">Brand ownership and history</p>
    <h2>Imperial, Middleby Corporation family member, California manufacturing since 1957</h2>
    <p>The current ownership chain: Imperial Commercial Cooking Equipment → Middleby Corporation. Per imperialrange.com manufacturer site: "Now a proud member of Middleby Corporation's family of foodservice brands, Imperial remains steadfast in developing quality, high-performance and durable kitchen equipment." Founded 1957 in Los Angeles. Manufacturing in Corona, California — the California factory remains operational under Middleby umbrella, which is structurally rare since most Middleby acquisitions consolidate to the Winston-Salem North Carolina distribution. Imperial's California production proximity is one of the brand's defining operational characteristics today, not an artifact of pre-acquisition independence.</p>
    <p>What that means practically for LA service: Imperial sits inside the same Middleby Corporation portfolio as Pitco (Bow NH commercial fryers), Frymaster (Shreveport LA commercial fryers, McDonald's OEM), Blodgett (Burlington VT deck and conveyor pizza ovens), TurboChef (rapid cook ovens), Wells (built-in cooking equipment), Holman (toasters and finishing equipment), and other Middleby cooking brands. For LA kitchens running multi-Middleby equipment configurations (common in commercial operations where Middleby-spec dealer packages came in as turnkey builds), one service relationship covers all of it. We carry the parts catalogs and the technician relationships across the full Middleby sibling list.</p>
    <p>The Middleby family positioning is a recent correction in our coverage. Older content across the SDAR site and across third-party service-provider sites referenced Imperial as "independent family-owned" — a claim that was inaccurate post-acquisition. We corrected the Imperial range and oven combo pages in commit d411d27 to align with the verified Middleby family ownership claim. The pillar deploys with the accurate framing from the start.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ── CORONA CA PARTS ADVANTAGE ── -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="eyebrow-sub">Corona CA parts proximity</p>
    <h2>The 60-mile factory advantage — what California-retained manufacturing means for LA service</h2>
    <p>When you call us for Imperial repair, parts ship from Corona — not Troy Ohio, not Vernon Hills Illinois, not Winston-Salem North Carolina, not Shreveport Louisiana. Imperial maintains California manufacturing even as part of Middleby's national family, and that proximity is a structural advantage specific to LA-area Imperial operators.</p>
    <p><strong>Typical lead times to LA from major commercial cooking brand factories:</strong></p>
    <ul class="check-list">
      <li><strong>Imperial — Corona CA, 60 miles from LA.</strong> 1 to 3 business days typical; same-day pickup possible for SFV/LA-proper service area when timing matters.</li>
      <li><strong>Hobart — Troy OH, 2,200 miles from LA.</strong> 3 to 5 days standard ground; 1 to 2 days expedited at premium.</li>
      <li><strong>Vulcan — ITW Chicago distribution, 2,000 miles.</strong> 3 to 5 days standard.</li>
      <li><strong>Frymaster — Shreveport LA (Louisiana, not Los Angeles), 1,600 miles.</strong> 3 to 5 days standard.</li>
      <li><strong>Pitco — Bow NH, 2,800 miles.</strong> 5 to 7 days standard.</li>
      <li><strong>Garland — Mississauga ON / Ali Group Winston-Salem NC, 2,500+ miles.</strong> 5 to 7 days standard via NC distribution.</li>
      <li><strong>Chinese OEM value-tier — overseas via WebstaurantStore or Restaurant Depot supply chains.</strong> 5 to 10 days, sometimes more.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>For LA operators on tight service timelines, the Corona California parts channel is a real operational benefit that we factor into the repair-vs-replace conversation. The proximity advantage compounds with Middleby family scale on national-distribution components — best of both: California factory parts for the Imperial-specific items, Middleby national distribution for cross-brand parts when the Imperial repair touches a Pitco or Frymaster system at the same kitchen.</p>
    <p>This is also why we recommend Imperial to operators evaluating value-tier commercial cooking purchases. The capital-cost advantage (15 to 25 percent below Vulcan or Garland equivalents) compounds with the service economics (faster parts, lower repair pricing tracking the same value-tier positioning). For mid-LA-market casual-dining, specialty-cuisine, and value-conscious institutional operations, Imperial economics work out favorably.</p>
  </div>
</section>

