<!-- ── SALAMANDER + CHEESEMELTER DEEP DIVE ── -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="eyebrow-sub">Salamanders and Cheesemelters</p>
    <h2>ISB salamanders and ICM cheesemelters — Imperial's LA Mexican/Latin restaurant signature</h2>
    <p>Two product lines that get less marketing attention than Imperial ranges but show up constantly in LA service. Both use infrared element architecture; both share the same primary aging failure (element burnout); both are operator-preferred value-tier choices versus the Vulcan and Star equivalents.</p>
    <p><strong>ISB infrared salamander broilers.</strong> Overhead infrared finishing units mounted above the work line for browning, melting, and au gratin applications. Common at sandwich shops finishing melts, French restaurants browning gratin programs, brunch concepts melting toppings, and steakhouse plating stations adding char finish to plated dishes. We frequently service ISB salamanders as paired equipment with IR-G range installations — same operator, same Imperial-spec build, both serviceable on one dispatch. Service pattern: infrared element burnout at year 4 to 8 ($240 to $420 parts-and-labor), gas valve at year 7 to 10, pilot assembly and thermocouple as paired wear items.</p>
    <p><strong>ICM cheesemelters.</strong> Imperial's specialty product that has unusually high LA concentration relative to national install base. ICM cheesemelters are heavily used in LA Mexican and Latin restaurants for nacho cheese, enchilada finishing, and taco operations where cheese-melting throughput matters. Pizza chains also run ICM for cheese topping applications. The high-cycle operational duty in this segment accelerates element wear — we see ICM cheesemelter calls at year 4 to 8 in heavy Mexican/Latin restaurant duty (versus 8 to 10 years in lower-volume operations). We carry ICM elements on the van for same-visit resolution.</p>
    <p>The ISB-to-ICM diagnostic relationship matters: both products use similar infrared element architecture, similar gas safety controls, similar service approaches. When we dispatch to a Latin restaurant with multiple Imperial pieces down, the diagnostic pattern carries across both categories — we typically resolve ISB and ICM service items together when both appear on the same call.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ── CHARBROILERS + GRIDDLES ── -->
<section class="section section-alt">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="eyebrow-sub">Charbroilers and Griddles</p>
    <h2>IRB charbroilers and ITG griddles — value-tier alternatives to Garland</h2>
    <p>Imperial's charbroiler and griddle lines occupy the value-tier mid-segment of the LA market — solid mid-market install base, less premium-branded than Garland Charbroilers GS-series or Vulcan equivalents, but reliable workhorses for casual-dining and mid-tier restaurant operations.</p>
    <p><strong>IRB radiant gas charbroilers.</strong> Lava-rock and radiant-plate configurations for mid-tier grill operations. Less prominent than Garland GS-series in the LA premium steakhouse cluster but consistent presence in value-tier and casual-dining grill programs. Service pattern: radiant burner ceramic crack at year 5 to 8 (replacement $280 to $480 — primary failure mode), burner orifice cleaning at year 4 to 6 ($180 to $280), gas valve replacement at year 7 to 10. Lava-rock variants require periodic lava replacement (operator-maintenance task, not service call typically).</p>
    <p><strong>ICRA char-radiant variants.</strong> Combined char-and-radiant configurations for operations needing both char-finish and radiant-cook capabilities in one unit. Service approach overlaps IRB; parts catalog differs.</p>
    <p><strong>ITG and ITHG gas griddles.</strong> 24-inch through 48-inch gas flat-tops for diner format operations, hotel breakfast service, brunch-heavy concepts, and hospital cafeteria breakfast lines. Service items: griddle surface re-polishing and re-seasoning at year 4 to 6 ($200 to $340), thermostat calibration drift correction ($200 to $340), gas valve replacement, burner orifice cleaning. Coastal griddles (Santa Monica, Manhattan Beach) corrode faster on burner orifices — we factor that into the service interval recommendation.</p>
    <p><strong>ED electric griddle variants.</strong> Electric flat-tops for kitchens without commercial gas supply or operator preference. Heating element replacement at year 5 to 8 ($300 to $520) is the primary aging failure mode. Element failure typically presents as one zone running cold while others stay hot — diagnostic confirms element open-circuit before swap.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ── SPECIALTY EQUIPMENT ── -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="eyebrow-sub">Specialty equipment</p>
    <h2>IPA pasta cookers, taco ranges, wok ranges — the LA cuisine-specialty Imperial moat</h2>
    <p>This is the product category that makes Imperial uniquely positioned in the LA market. Imperial built specialty cuisine equipment that other commercial cooking manufacturers don't bother with — taco ranges sized for high-volume tortilla production, wok ranges with proper BTU output for Cantonese cooking, IPA pasta cookers tuned for Italian operations. National brands like Hobart and Vulcan don't cover these segments. Imperial does, and LA's cuisine diversity makes it the operator-preferred default in this space.</p>
    <p><strong>IPA pasta cookers.</strong> Concentrated heavily in the LA Italian restaurant cluster — Beverly Hills, West Hollywood, Santa Monica trattoria scene, plus DTLA Italian concepts. Auto-fill solenoid failure ($240 to $380) is the primary IPA service item — solenoid valve sticks open or fails to open, causing water overflow or underfill. High-limit thermostat ($240 to $380) is the secondary aging item. We service IPA pasta cookers regularly across the LA Italian restaurant network.</p>
    <p><strong>Taco ranges.</strong> Imperial taco ranges (high-BTU burner array sized for tortilla production throughput) show up at independent taquerias, family-owned Mexican restaurants, and mid-market Mexican chains across East LA, San Fernando Valley, South LA, and DTLA Hispanic restaurant clusters. Service items match standard range patterns plus higher-frequency burner orifice cleaning given the high-cycle tortilla production duty.</p>
    <p><strong>Wok ranges.</strong> Asian restaurant install base — Koreatown Korean BBQ corridor, Chinatown operations, Asian-fusion mid-market concepts. Imperial wok ranges provide the BTU output that proper Cantonese cooking requires (200,000+ BTU per burner on the heavy-duty configurations). Service items include burner ring replacement at high cycle counts, water-cooled jacket maintenance on cooled-edge variants, gas valve service.</p>
    <p><strong>Stock pot ranges.</strong> Asian restaurant soup specialists, broth-program operations, sauce-base prep stations. Heavy-duty low-burner configurations for large-pot work.</p>
    <p><strong>Range hoods.</strong> Imperial also manufactures hood systems integrated with the range product line. Hood blower motor service, capture/makeup-air balance issues, hood thermostat service on heated-lamp variants.</p>
    <p><strong>Accessories.</strong> Hot plates, equipment stands, backguards and shelves — common Imperial-spec build accessories that we service alongside the primary cooking equipment.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ── LA INSTALL MAP ── -->
<section class="section section-alt">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="eyebrow-sub">LA install map</p>
    <h2>Where Imperial equipment lives across LA's cuisine clusters</h2>
    <p>Imperial concentrates in six distinct LA operator clusters. Each has a characteristic equipment profile.</p>

    <h3 class="line-h3">Mexican / Latin restaurants — Imperial's biggest LA install base</h3>
    <p>LA Mexican and Latin restaurant scene is the highest-density Imperial concentration in the city. Independent taquerias, family-owned restaurants, mid-market Mexican chains across East LA, San Fernando Valley, South LA, plus DTLA's Hispanic restaurant cluster. Equipment mix: Imperial taco ranges + ICM cheesemelters + IRB charbroilers + IR-G ranges. We dispatch to this cluster regularly; ICM cheesemelter service alone is a meaningful weekly route.</p>

    <h3 class="line-h3">Italian restaurants — IPA pasta cookers concentration</h3>
    <p>Beverly Hills, West Hollywood, Santa Monica trattoria scene + DTLA Italian concepts. IPA pasta cookers + ICM cheesemelters + IRB charbroilers + IR-G ranges. The Italian-restaurant install base is the IPA pasta cooker territory specifically — Imperial sells more IPA units into the LA Italian restaurant market than any other US metro per industry observation.</p>

    <h3 class="line-h3">Asian restaurants — wok and stock pot ranges</h3>
    <p>LA Korean BBQ corridor (Koreatown), Chinatown operations, Asian-fusion mid-market concepts. Imperial wok ranges + stock pot ranges are operator-preferred specialty equipment in this segment — national brands don't cover the BTU requirements properly. Imperial does.</p>

    <h3 class="line-h3">Steakhouse / grill concepts — mid-tier (IRB + ISB)</h3>
    <p>Mid-tier steakhouses and grill concepts run IRB charbroilers + ISB salamanders. Lower install base than Garland GS-series in the premium steakhouse cluster (Mastro's, Ruth's Chris, Wolfgang's run Garland) but Imperial holds the value-tier-steakhouse and casual-grill segment consistently.</p>

    <h3 class="line-h3">Mid-size independent restaurants — Imperial's pricing-position core market</h3>
    <p>Imperial's 15-to-25-percent pricing advantage versus Vulcan and Garland makes it the default mid-size independent operator choice. Spans all of LA's mid-market restaurant scene — casual-dining, neighborhood concepts, family operations, smaller chains. Distribution wider than premium-brand competitors precisely because of price + value-tier positioning.</p>

    <h3 class="line-h3">K-12 and institutional kitchens — HD ranges + ICV convection</h3>
    <p>LAUSD central kitchens, community college dining operations, hospital food service production lines. Imperial HD ranges + ICV convection ovens at lower-budget institutional operations where Vulcan or Garland premium specs would be over-specified.</p>
  </div>
</section>

