<!-- ── USED IMPERIAL MARKET ── -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="eyebrow-sub">Used Imperial market</p>
    <h2>Restaurant liquidation pipeline — used Imperial in LA</h2>
    <p>LA's restaurant turnover rate produces a steady stream of used Imperial equipment hitting auction and liquidation channels. Restaurant Equipment World, IRS auctions, RestaurantEquipment.com listings, plus the direct-dealer-liquidation channel — all of these put used Imperial in front of LA operators at meaningful discount to new pricing.</p>
    <p><strong>Typical LA used-Imperial economics:</strong></p>
    <ul class="check-list">
      <li>Used IR-G restaurant ranges run $800 to $2,800 (versus $4,000 to $8,000 new).</li>
      <li>Used ICV convection ovens run $1,200 to $3,500 (versus $5,000 to $10,000 new).</li>
      <li>Used ICM cheesemelters run $400 to $1,200 (versus $1,800 to $4,000 new).</li>
      <li>Used IPA pasta cookers run $600 to $1,800 (versus $3,500 to $7,000 new).</li>
      <li>Used IRB charbroilers run $500 to $1,500 (versus $2,500 to $5,500 new).</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The used-Imperial price-to-new ratio is favorable relative to premium-brand alternatives. Imperial's mid-tier purchase pricing keeps new prices accessible, which means the used market discount is less dramatic in absolute dollar terms — but the operating cost advantage versus buying new still works out for value-conscious operators.</p>
    <p>We don't sell Imperial equipment. We service it regardless of how you acquired it. The honest framing: if you bought used at auction and you need a tech who knows the Corona California parts pipeline, the SIT-style gas safety architecture, the ICM cheesemelter element replacement procedure, we are that tech. We won't shame you for not buying new from Costco-foodservice-distributor channels.</p>
    <p>The serviceability math typically works out. Imperial's California factory parts continuity is good even on 10 to 12-year-old units — Corona maintains current parts inventory for legacy product lines longer than most consolidator-owned brand factories. A $400 repair on a $1,200 used ICM cheesemelter that retails $3,000 new is a clear operational win. We give the honest assessment after the $120 diagnostic and we will tell you straight if a unit is past economic repair.</p>
  </div>
</section>

